A||||11||LZeitgeist||ARGH!!!! Ratzin-fratzin SQUEAKY BELT!!!||||1||
Z||000000||LZeitgeist||09-02-2001||09:39 PM||||O.K.... a lot of us here have V6's. And as we *all* know, the belt squeaks whenever it dang well pleases. <P>Has anyone here, with the large number of exceedingly skilled craftspeople here, ever attempted to come up with either A) a spring-loaded belt tensioner similar to what is used ont he 4-cylinder engines, or B) a spring-loaded alternator bracket that could be retrofitted on the existing V6 engine? A slightly larger belt could be used, and theoretically, the maintained tension throughout the life of the belt would prevent it from squeaking. (I mean, is that why the 4-cyl belts never squeak? Because of the tensioner keeping the belt tight?)<P>Man, I *know* I'm not the only one who gets completely disgusted with looking like I'm in a nifty sports car, and sounding like I'm in a rattletrap Edsel... anybody with applicable technical experience got any ideas?<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001||||reg||1
Z||000001||XDrewX||09-02-2001||09:45 PM||||I think it should be possible to put a tensioner on the V6.   My 87 pontiac 6000, had the 2.8 V6 and it had a belt tensioner.<BR>Hmmmmmm, it also had power steering.<P>I wish i still had that car, I would love to try to do that. I could change the belt on that car in 3 minutes. I swear that the 2.8 in that car was more powerful than the 2.8 in my fiero.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||LZeitgeist||09-02-2001||09:48 PM||||Ooo.. that sounds promising... might be a place to start...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fast Richard||09-02-2001||10:09 PM||||I'm not really convinced that a tensioner would have much effect. Remember, you can get these belts tighter that a fiddlestring and they still squeal! My idea is that the alternator pulley is too small, so there is not enough contact with the belt. I would be willing to try a pulley that's a bit larger in diameter, if one could be found. I don't think charging would suffer.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LZeitgeist||09-02-2001||10:19 PM||||Well, the thing is (at least in my experience) that when the belt is freshly-tightened, it doesn't squeal... it's only after the engine compartment heat and/or water from the road begin to affect it again that it starts squeaking... but my case may be different from others, so I'm open to suggestions... I'm just *very* tired of having to retighten this dumb thing so dang often, when belts on other cars last a great deal longer without making a peep. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||09-02-2001||11:26 PM||||I think most of the FWD 2.8's have reverse rotation water pumps, which enables them to route the belt over the top from aft to fore, then going to a tensioner and back to the alternator.  The 3.8 I looked at in a Bonneville the other day did this, with or without AC (compressor in same location as ours, only one long belt for everything).  If a reverse rotation pump would fit on a Fiero, maybe one could put a tensioner where the AC compressor is (at the cost of AC, of course).  <BR>I think the fiero may do better with a V-belt, since it's such a short triangular run.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||09-03-2001||07:15 AM||||I dont have a problem with squeeling, mine only does it when its been parked out in the rain, and then only a few seconds. I do have a problem in a heavy rain, going thru puddles, it slips for a few minutes (no charge) then grabs again. Too bad they dont have toothed belts and pullies.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||09-03-2001||11:23 AM||||Try some parafin (I spelled that wrong, didn't I?) or some of that orange, gritty hand cleaner.  <B>DO NOT</B> use spray one belt dressing.  That will only make the belt slicker (which is what you're trying to avoid).  I've used both parraffinn (I know I spelled that wrong, but I'm covering my bases) and that orange, gritty hand cleaner.  Both work well but don't last forever.  After a few weeks with some pretty good rain, you have to start all over again.  The other suggestion is to break down and have a shop install your next belt.  That's what I did and they tighten up my belts as often as needed free of charge.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||09-03-2001||11:46 AM||||True, Monk, but if they install a crappy belt, you'll just keep on taking it back until the belt finally sh!ts itself 8 miles from home, leaving you to limp it home with all of your coolant boiling out. Grrr. Been there, done that.<P>Make sure you get a good belt in the first place. There is a difference. Buy Gates belts and only Gates belts! I've never had a Gates belt squeak on me<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000009||theogre||09-03-2001||05:31 PM||||Repeated belt issues like this can also be caused by pulley wear. If the shape of the goove has gone out of spec then no belt will ever last.<P>read my serpent belt article. allot of what is in there applies to all belts.<P>Watch what you wash the car with... allot of car washing product could be bad for the belt. One step products that contain polishes are an issue. So are the spray/foam waxes at comercial carwash barns.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||09-05-2001||07:51 AM||||I agree with Stimpy.  Gates belts are the only brand I've had no trouble with.  All other  brands I've tried eventually squeal.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Wolfhound||09-05-2001||07:58 AM||||It seems to always take a second tightning for me when installing new belts. seems they have to run in a bit.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
